@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant — same exchange as choreography vs collaboration.
'   usecase:      Note the viewpoint difference (participant-to-participant vs independent pools + message flows).
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       business-process
' ============================================================================
title Example BPMN Choreography - Choreography vs Collaboration (Variant)

' Same Buyer/Seller exchange (order -> ack -> goods -> payment) shown two ways.
' Left: choreography — no pools, banded tasks describe the protocol.
' Right: collaboration — independent pools with internal processes + message flows.

' --- Part 1: Choreography -------------------------------------------------
usecase " " as ChStart
usecase " " as ChEnd

package "Place Order\n[ Buyer | Seller ]" as CT1
package "Acknowledge\n[ Seller | Buyer ]" as CT2
package "Ship Goods\n[ Seller | Buyer ]" as CT3
package "Send Payment\n[ Buyer | Seller ]" as CT4

ChStart --> CT1
CT1 --> CT2
CT2 --> CT3
CT3 --> CT4
CT4 --> ChEnd

note top of CT1
  <b>Choreography viewpoint</b>
  No pools. Each task IS the
  exchange, banded by who starts
  (Buyer, white) and who responds
  (Seller, grey). The focus is
  the CONTRACT between participants.
end note

' --- Part 2: Collaboration ------------------------------------------------
' Independent pools with their own internal flows + dashed message flows.
rectangle "**Buyer**" as P1 {
  usecase " " as Bs
  rectangle "Submit Order" as Bt1
  rectangle "Pay" as Bt2
  rectangle "Receive Goods" as Bt3
  usecase " " as Be
  Bs --> Bt1
  Bt1 --> Bt2
  Bt1 --> Bt3
  Bt3 --> Be
  Bt2 --> Be
}

rectangle "**Seller**" as P2 {
  usecase " " as Ss
  rectangle "Accept Order" as St1
  rectangle "Ship" as St2
  usecase " " as Se
  Ss --> St1
  St1 --> St2
  St2 --> Se
}

Bt1 ..> St1 : "Order"
St1 ..> Bt1 : "Ack"
St2 ..> Bt3 : "Goods"
Bt2 ..> St2 : "Payment"

note top of P1
  <b>Collaboration viewpoint</b>
  Independent pools. Each pool owns
  its internal process; message flows
  (dashed) connect them. The focus is
  each participant's WORK, glued by
  messages.
end note

note bottom of P2
  <b>When to use which</b>
  Choreography: protocol/contract
  design, multi-party orchestration,
  standards (e.g. WS-CDL feel).
  Collaboration: each party's
  internal behavior + handoffs.
end note

@enduml
